addresses.comment:
**`GER = { ... }` read as a condition: is the scope's country this one.** 0x5E8960 to two `ret 4` at 0x5E898D and 0x5E89AB - one function, the `je 0x5E8990` at 0x5E8976 jumping past the first exit. 10 int3 above; only **2** below, so the boundary is one the 'run of three int3' rule walks past.

    id = this->tag.id (+0x44)
    if (this->arg_is_this (+0x18))      id = scope->this_scope (+0x38)->country_id (+0x14)
    else if (this->arg_is_from (+0x19)) id = scope->this_scope (+0x38)->from_country_id (+0x1C)
    return scope->country_id (+0x14) == id

Three things worth having. **The comparison is by index only** - the three tag characters at +0x40 are copied into a stack slot in every arm and nothing reads them again, so they are carried for display and not for the test. **THIS and FROM are both read through `scope->this_scope`**, not off the scope itself, taking +0x10/+0x14 for THIS and +0x18/+0x1C for FROM. And the two flags at +0x18/+0x19 are CTrigger's own, so `THIS = { ... }` and a literal tag are the same class.
